README for OpenBFDD
*******************

=====================
+ Use the Wiki
=====================
This document contains only very minimal information. More detailed 
information can be obtained on the projects GitHub wiki, currently located 
at: https://github.com/dyninc/OpenBFDD/wiki


================
+ Intro
================

OpenBFDD is an open source package that provides a software based 
implantation of the Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) protocol. It 
is designed to run on a server. Its primary purpose is to allow the 
administrator to quickly take the server off-line when the core service, 
such as a bind or unbound server, goes down. This is done by providing a 
BFD "beacon", bfdd-beacon, that can communicate with a properly 
configured, BFD enabled, router. A control utility, bfdd-control, can be 
used to configure the running bfdd-beacon daemon, and to change the state 
of its BFD sessions. 


================
+ Build
================

OpenBFDD uses the GNU autotools build system. From the source directory:

    ./autogen.sh        # generate the configure script (needs autoconf/automake)
    ./configure         # detect the toolchain and create the Makefile
    make                # build bfdd-beacon and bfdd-control

Useful configure options:

    ./configure --help                 # list all options
    ./configure --enable-debug         # build with extra debug checks (BFD_DEBUG)

The build produces two binaries in the source directory:

    bfdd-beacon         # the BFD daemon
    bfdd-control        # the control/configuration utility

To install them system wide:

    sudo make install


================
+ Run
================

Start the daemon (single-hop mode, the default). It listens for BFD
control packets on UDP port 3784:

    sudo ./bfdd-beacon --listen=0.0.0.0

Common bfdd-beacon options:

    --listen=<ip>       # address to listen on; 0.0.0.0 / :: means all
    --control=<ip:port> # control channel; may be repeated
    --nofork            # run in the foreground (do not daemonize)
    --multihop          # RFC 5883 multi-hop mode (see below)

Once the daemon is running, use bfdd-control to drive it:

    ./bfdd-control connect <localIP> <remoteIP>   # start an active session
    ./bfdd-control allow <remoteIP>               # accept a passive session
    ./bfdd-control status                         # show all sessions
    ./bfdd-control session <id> set ...           # change session parameters

Multi-hop mode (RFC 5883):

By default bfdd-beacon runs in single-hop mode (RFC 5881): it uses UDP
port 3784 and requires received packets to have a TTL/Hop Limit of 255
(the GTSM anti-spoofing check). Passing --multihop switches the whole
instance to multi-hop mode:

    sudo ./bfdd-beacon --listen=0.0.0.0 --multihop

In multi-hop mode all sessions listen and send on UDP port 4784, and the
TTL/Hop Limit check is not applied (it does not work across multiple hops).
The bfdd-control commands are unchanged. A single bfdd-beacon instance uses
one mode for all of its sessions; single-hop and multi-hop sessions can not
be mixed in the same instance. The remote peer (router/switch) must be
configured for the matching mode and port, or no session will come up.


================
+ Test
================

A local smoke test is provided to verify the single-hop and multi-hop
packet paths on a single host using loopback alias addresses:

    sudo ./tests/smoke-multihop.sh

It starts two bfdd-beacon instances, opens sessions between them, and uses
tcpdump to confirm that single-hop traffic uses port 3784 and multi-hop
traffic (with --multihop) uses port 4784, and that the sessions reach the
Up state. It prints "ALL CHECKS PASSED" on success.

Requirements: Linux, root (to add loopback aliases and capture traffic),
tcpdump, and the built bfdd-beacon / bfdd-control binaries. The binary and
address defaults can be overridden with environment variables, for example:

    sudo BEACON=./bfdd-beacon CONTROL=./bfdd-control UP_TIMEOUT=20 \
        ./tests/smoke-multihop.sh

Note: this is a control-plane smoke test on a single host. It validates the
daemon's port/TTL behaviour only; it does not validate the real network path
to an external router or switch.


